<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tamil Nadu</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tamil Nadu is a state of south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coromandel-coast">Coromandel Coast</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Chennai</a>. It formed the core of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamilakam">Tamilakam</a>, ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chera-dynasty">Cheras</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Cholas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandyas</a>, and is known for its great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temples and classical Tamil literature. The state took its present borders as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madras-state">Madras State</a> in 1956 on linguistic lines and was renamed Tamil Nadu in 1969.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu.jpg" alt="Tamil Nadu" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: pnglife (Flickr) · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mamallapuram_view.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Tamil Nadu covers 130,058 square kilometres on the southeastern coast of the Indian peninsula. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-ghats">Western Ghats</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan-plateau">Deccan Plateau</a> lie to the west, the Eastern Ghats to the north and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-bengal">Bay of Bengal</a> to the east, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mannar">Gulf of Mannar</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palk-strait">Palk Strait</a> to the southeast and the Laccadive Sea at the southern cape. The river Kaveri crosses the state. The name means the land of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamils">Tamils</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Excavations at Keezhadi have revealed a large urban settlement, with the earliest artefact dated to 580 BCE. Inscriptions at Adichanallur use Tamil Brahmi, dated to the 5th century BCE. In the Sangam period, from about 500 BCE to 300 CE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamilakam">Tamilakam</a> was ruled by three monarchical states: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chera-dynasty">Cheras</a> in the west, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandyas</a> in the south and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Cholas</a> in the Kaveri delta. Their kings patronised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedic-religion">Vedic religion</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a> and sponsored some of the earliest Tamil literature. The oldest surviving work is the Tolkāppiyam, a Tamil grammar.</p><p>The Pallavas, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanchipuram">Kanchipuram</a> as their capital, were noted for their patronage of architecture, and the gopuram, the tower at the entrance of a temple, originated with them. They built the rock-cut monuments of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamallapuram">Mahabalipuram</a> and temples at Kanchipuram, and were in constant conflict with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Cholas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandyas</a> until the Chola prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aditya-i">Aditya I</a> defeated them in the 9th century. The Vijayanagara kingdom, founded in 1336, conquered the entire Tamil country by about 1370 and ruled for almost two centuries, until its defeat at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-talikota">Battle of Talikota</a> in 1565. Its military governors, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai-nayak-dynasty">Nayaks of Madurai</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanjavur">Thanjavur</a>, then took control and rebuilt temples such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meenakshi-temple">Meenakshi Temple</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai">Madurai</a>.</p><p>European colonisation began with trading ports in the 17th century, and the British controlled much of the state as part of the Madras Presidency for two centuries. After 1947 it became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madras-state">Madras State</a>, which lost Andhra in 1953 and took its present shape when states were redrawn on linguistic lines in 1956. It was renamed Tamil Nadu on 14 January 1969.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Tamil Nadu is the southernmost state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Chennai</a>, and 72.1 million people at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 2011. Tamil, the first language recognised as classical in India, is its official language, and 87.6 per cent of its people follow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a>. It has four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Sites</a> and is known for its great temples and its classical Tamil literature.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tamil Nadu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After a first period of power in the early medieval centuries, they were conquered by the Chola Empire in the tenth century and spent most of the eleventh and twelfth centuries as subordinate or rebellious princes in the far south of <b>Tamil Nadu</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/later-pandyan-empire">Later Pandyan Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>They emerged as the Satavahana dynasty and its successor in the east, the Ikshvaku dynasty, declined, and their heartland was Tondaimandalam, the country around Kanchipuram in the north of present-day <b>Tamil Nadu</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pallava-dynasty">Pallava dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Travancore takes its name from Thiruvithamcode, in the present Kanyakumari district of <b>Tamil Nadu</b>, contracted to Thiruvankode and anglicised by the English.</blockquote><a href="/empire/travancore">Travancore · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Vijayanagara architecture, with its tall gateway towers, pillared halls carved with horses and mythical beasts and large temple-city complexes, set a style followed across the south, and many temples in <b>Tamil Nadu</b> and Andhra Pradesh were rebuilt or enlarged under its rule.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Inscriptions suggest a significant presence in Karnataka, especially in Kolar district, and in parts of <b>Tamil Nadu</b>.</blockquote><a href="/religion/ajivika">Ājīvika · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>In the south, Jainism flourished in Karnataka and <b>Tamil Nadu</b> in the first millennium CE under dynasties such as the Western Gangas, whose minister Chavundaraya had the great statue of Bahubali carved at Shravanabelagola around 983; Jain monks contributed greatly to early literature in Tamil and Kannada.</blockquote><a href="/religion/jainism">Jainism · Spread</a></li></ul></section>
